.TH CYAML_DELETE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cyaml_delete \- remove and free the DOM node at a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

ccol_retval_t cyaml_delete(cyaml root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The macro navigates to the parent of the node that the path addresses,
removes the child, and frees the whole subtree of that child
recursively.  The path uses the same dot-separated syntax as cyaml_get
and cyaml_set.

For a dictionary parent, the leaf component is the key to remove.  For
a list parent, the leaf component must be a '#N' token; for example,
"#0" removes the first element.

The macro splits the path at the LAST dot that carries no escape, so
the path "a.b.c" targets the key "c" inside the node at "a.b".  When
the path holds no dot, root is the parent.

As in cyaml_get and cyaml_set, the escape sequences '\e.' and '\e\e'
give a literal dot or a literal backslash in a key name.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_retval_t.  The macro returns ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args comes back in six cases: a NULL root, a NULL path, an
empty path, a component that is not valid for the node that it
addresses, bad '#N' index syntax, and an empty component.  A component
that is not '#N' on a list is one invalid case, and any component that
the macro finds under a scalar is another.  An empty component is
nothing between two dots, a leading dot, or a trailing dot.  Any
position in the path can give this code, not only the leaf.
ccol_key_not_found comes back when a component with correct syntax (a
dictionary key or a '#N' index) is absent, at any position, and also
when a list index with correct syntax is out of range.
ccol_not_enough_memory comes back on an internal allocation failure.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cyaml_delete(doc, "server.debug");
cyaml_delete(doc, "hosts.#0");
cyaml_delete(doc, "users.alice.address");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_get (3),
.BR cyaml_set (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_remove (3),
.BR cyaml_list_remove (3)
